{#
    The tab icon, for every page that has a <head> of its own.

    Included rather than repeated because a page that omits it does not merely
    look unbranded: the browser falls back to requesting /favicon.ico, which
    matches no route, and Symfony logs the 404 at error level. The admin log
    filled with "No route found for GET /favicon.ico" every time someone opened
    "show original", which is a real fault report about nothing.

    This used to be an emoji in a data URI — ✉️ — which cost no request but was
    not the product's mark, and rendered as whatever envelope the reader's
    operating system happens to draw. A tab strip is where people find plMail
    among thirty other tabs, and the thing they look for is the icon.

    Now the real mark, in the same two forms every other icon here takes:
    an SVG that stays sharp at 16px and any pixel ratio, and the 192px PNG for
    anything that will not take an SVG favicon. Browsers pick the first `rel`
    they understand, so the SVG is listed first.

    The SVG comes from a route rather than a file, because the mark's
    colourway is a per-user setting now (Settings → Appearance → Logo) and a
    static file can only wear one. Anonymous pages get the product default
    from the same route; /icons/favicon.svg still exists, painted in that
    default, for anything that fetches icons without a session.
#}
{# The style value rides in the URL as a cache-buster. Browsers keep a
   favicon cache of their own that ignores ordinary revalidation until a hard
   reload — the ETag alone left people staring at the old colourway. A new
   style is a new URL, and the appearance page swaps this link's href live on
   pick (see pickLogo), so the tab follows the click. The server ignores the
   parameter: the session decides the colourway, v only names it. #}
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ path('app_branding_favicon', { v: appearance().effectiveLogoStyle.value }) }}">
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192" href="/icons/icon-192.png">
